<%# =============================================================================
    APPLICATION LAYOUT
    This is the main page "wrapper" — Rails automatically renders this file
    around EVERY view in the app (unless a controller says otherwise). Think
    of it as the picture frame: the <html>/<head>/<body> boilerplate, the
    navigation bar, and the footer live here ONCE, and each individual page
    (dashboard, settings, alert rules, etc.) only has to provide the content
    that goes in the middle.

    This file's extension is ".html.erb" — ERB (Embedded RuBy) lets you mix
    Ruby code into an HTML file. Two tag forms matter throughout this file:
    `<% % >` runs Ruby but outputs nothing to the page (used for logic like
    if/loops); `<%= % >` runs Ruby AND inserts its result into the HTML as
    text. `<%# % >` — like this very comment block — is an ERB comment: it is
    stripped out entirely before the page is sent to a browser, so it's safe
    to write plain English here without it showing up on the site.
============================================================================= %>
<%# `<!DOCTYPE html>` is not Rails/ERB syntax — it's plain HTML, telling the
    browser "parse this as modern HTML5" (without it, browsers can fall back
    to quirky, inconsistent rendering rules from the 1990s). %>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<%# `<html lang="en">` opens the root element of the whole page; `lang="en"`
    tells browsers/screen readers/search engines the page's text is English.
    Every other tag in this file nests inside this one, closed by `</html>`
    at the very bottom of the file. %>
<html lang="en">
<%# `<head>` holds metadata and asset links that aren't visible page
    content — page title, character encoding, stylesheets, scripts. Nothing
    inside `<head>` is rendered as visible page body text (except the
    browser tab title). %>
<head>
  <%# `<meta charset="UTF-8">` tells the browser which text-encoding to use
      when interpreting the bytes of this page, so special characters
      (accents, emoji, the 🏪 used below) display correctly instead of as
      garbled symbols. %>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <%# `<meta name="viewport" ...>` tells mobile browsers to render the page
      at the device's actual width (instead of zoomed out to fit a
      desktop-sized layout) and to start at 100% zoom — this is what makes
      the page look right on phones. %>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <%# Page title — uses controller-set @page_title or falls back to "StorageFinder" %>
  <%# `<title>` sets the text shown in the browser tab/window title and in
      bookmarks. `<%= current_page_title % >` calls a helper method (defined
      elsewhere in the app) and prints whatever string it returns right
      here, between the `<title>` and `</title>` tags. %>
  <title><%= current_page_title %></title>

  <%# PWA manifest — tells a browser this app can be installed
      ("add to home screen"), and where to find its icon/name/colors.
      `pwa_manifest_path` is the named route added in config/routes.rb,
      pointing at app/views/pwa/manifest.json.erb. %>
  <link rel="manifest" href="<%= pwa_manifest_path %>">

  <%# Tailwind CSS — utility classes for styling %>
  <%# `stylesheet_link_tag` is a Rails helper that outputs an HTML `<link>`
      tag pointing at the compiled "tailwind" stylesheet, so the browser
      downloads and applies all the CSS classes used throughout this app
      (like `bg-gray-950` a few lines below). `"data-turbo-track": "reload"`
      is a Turbo (Rails' page-acceleration library) instruction: if this
      stylesheet's content changes between page visits, Turbo does a full
      page reload instead of silently keeping stale CSS. %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag "tailwind", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>

  <%# Leaflet — required CSS for the facility map on the dashboard (see
      app/javascript/controllers/facility_map_controller.js and
      config/importmap.rb's "leaflet" pin). Vendored locally the same way
      as tailwind above, rather than pulled from a CDN. %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag "leaflet", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>

  <%# JavaScript — Stimulus + Turbo + our custom JS %>
  <%# `javascript_importmap_tags` is a Rails helper (from the "importmap"
      approach to JS, which lets browsers load JavaScript modules by name
      without a build step) that outputs all the `<script>` tags needed to
      boot the app's JavaScript — including Turbo (fast page navigation)
      and Stimulus (small JS behavior "controllers" attached to HTML). %>
  <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>

  <%# ActionCable — WebSocket connection for live crawl progress %>
  <%# `action_cable_meta_tag` outputs a `<meta>` tag containing the
      WebSocket URL that ActionCable's JavaScript client reads to open a
      persistent, two-way connection to the server — used here so the page
      can receive live updates (e.g. crawl progress) without refreshing. %>
  <%= action_cable_meta_tag %>

  <%# CSRF token — needed for all form submissions %>
  <%# `csrf_meta_tags` outputs `<meta>` tags containing a secret,
      per-session token. Rails checks this token on every form submission
      to prove the request really came from this app's own pages (a
      defense against Cross-Site Request Forgery attacks, where a
      malicious site tries to submit a form on your behalf). Without this
      tag present, Rails would reject non-GET form submissions. %>
  <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
<%# `</head>` closes the `<head>` element opened above. %>
</head>

<%# `<body class="...">` opens the visible page area. The `class="..."`
    attribute is a space-separated list of Tailwind CSS utility classes:
    `bg-gray-950` sets a near-black background, `text-gray-100` sets
    near-white default text, `min-h-screen` makes the page at least as
    tall as the browser viewport (so a short page's background still fills
    the screen), and `font-mono` sets a monospace font for the whole app. %>
<body class="bg-gray-950 text-gray-100 min-h-screen font-mono">

  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# NAVIGATION BAR %>
  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# `<nav>` is the semantic HTML tag for a block of navigation links (helps
      screen readers and search engines understand this section's purpose).
      `bg-gray-900` = dark background, `border-b border-gray-800` = a thin
      bottom border, `sticky top-0` = the nav bar sticks to the top of the
      viewport and stays visible while the page scrolls, `z-50` = a high
      stacking order so it renders above other page content instead of
      being covered by it. %>
  <nav class="bg-gray-900 border-b border-gray-800 sticky top-0 z-50">
    <%# This inner `<div>` centers the nav bar's contents and caps their
        width: `max-w-screen-xl` limits how wide it stretches on large
        screens, `mx-auto` centers it horizontally, `px-4` adds left/right
        padding, and `flex items-center justify-between h-14` arranges its
        children (logo on the left, links on the right) in a single row,
        vertically centered, at a fixed height of 14 Tailwind spacing units. %>
    <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 flex items-center justify-between h-14">

      <%# Logo / App name — links back to the dashboard %>
      <%# `link_to` is a Rails helper that outputs an HTML `<a>` (anchor/
          link) tag. This call form — `link_to path, options do ... end` —
          passes a BLOCK as the link's inner content instead of a plain
          text string, so we can put multiple HTML elements (the emoji span
          and the text span below) inside one clickable link. `root_path`
          is the URL for the app's home page (defined by the routes file).
          The `class:` string is more Tailwind styling for the link. %>
      <%= link_to root_path, class: "flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold text-lg hover:text-emerald-300 transition-colors" do %>
        <%# A `<span>` is a generic inline HTML container — here it wraps
            the store emoji at a larger text size. %>
        <span class="text-2xl">🏪</span>
        <%# A second `<span>` holding the plain app name text. %>
        <span>StorageFinder</span>
      <%# `<% end % >` closes the `do ... end` block passed to `link_to`
          above — in ERB, Ruby's block/if/loop keywords still have to
          balance even though they're split across separate `<% % >` tags. %>
      <% end %>

      <%# Navigation links %>
      <%# A plain `<div>` used purely for layout: `flex items-center gap-6`
          lays its children out in a row with consistent spacing between
          them. %>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
        <%# `link_to "Dashboard", root_path, class: "..."` is the simpler
            form of `link_to`: first argument is the visible link text,
            second is the destination URL/path, third is HTML attributes.
            This one links to the home page. %>
        <%= link_to "Dashboard", root_path,
              class: "text-sm text-gray-300 hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors" %>

        <%# Links to the Alert Rules page; `alert_rules_path` is a
            Rails-generated URL helper based on the app's routes. %>
        <%= link_to "Alert Rules", alert_rules_path,
              class: "text-sm text-gray-300 hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors" %>

        <%# Links to the Settings page; `settings_path` is likewise a
            routes-generated URL helper. %>
        <%= link_to "Settings", settings_path,
              class: "text-sm text-gray-300 hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors" %>

        <%# Links to the API Keys admin page; api_keys_path is a
            routes-generated URL helper for the `resources :api_keys`
            declaration in config/routes.rb. %>
        <%= link_to "API Keys", api_keys_path,
              class: "text-sm text-gray-300 hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors" %>
      <%# Closes the `<div class="flex items-center gap-6">` that wraps the
          four navigation links above. %>
      </div>
    <%# Closes the centering `<div class="max-w-screen-xl ...">` opened
        above, which wraps both the logo and the nav links. %>
    </div>
  <%# Closes the `<nav>` element that opened this whole navigation bar
      section. %>
  </nav>

  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# FLASH MESSAGES (success / error banners) %>
  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# `flash` is a Rails mechanism for passing short, one-time messages from
      a controller action to the very next page rendered (commonly used
      after a redirect, e.g. "Settings saved!"). `flash[:notice]` reads the
      "notice" (success-style) message, if any was set. `.present?` is a
      Rails helper meaning "not nil and not empty" — true only when there's
      an actual message to show. `<% if ... % >` runs Ruby logic but prints
      nothing itself; the HTML below it only renders when the condition is
      true. %>
  <% if flash[:notice].present? %>
    <%# This banner `<div>` only appears when a notice message is present.
        `bg-emerald-900/50` = a semi-transparent dark-green background (the
        `/50` is Tailwind's opacity modifier), plus green border/text
        colors — styled to read as a "success" banner. `role="alert"` is an
        HTML accessibility attribute telling screen readers to announce
        this content immediately, since it's a transient status message. %>
    <div class="bg-emerald-900/50 border-b border-emerald-700 text-emerald-300 px-4 py-3 text-sm" role="alert">
      <%# The ✓ character is a plain literal checkmark, followed by the
          actual message text printed via `<%= flash[:notice] % >`. %>
      ✓ <%= flash[:notice] %>
    </div>
  <%# Closes the `<% if flash[:notice].present? % >` conditional above. %>
  <% end %>

  <%# Same pattern as above, but for `flash[:alert]` — Rails' conventional
      key for error/warning-style one-time messages. %>
  <% if flash[:alert].present? %>
    <%# Styled as a "danger" banner: dark-red background, red border/text. %>
    <div class="bg-red-900/50 border-b border-red-700 text-red-300 px-4 py-3 text-sm" role="alert">
      <%# The ✗ character is a plain literal "X" mark preceding the message. %>
      ✗ <%= flash[:alert] %>
    </div>
  <%# Closes the `<% if flash[:alert].present? % >` conditional above. %>
  <% end %>

  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# MAIN CONTENT AREA %>
  <%# yield renders whatever the current page's view outputs %>
  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# `<main>` is the semantic HTML tag marking the page's primary content
      region (as opposed to nav/footer/sidebars). The Tailwind classes cap
      its width, center it, and add horizontal/vertical padding, matching
      the nav bar's layout above. %>
  <main class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 py-6">
    <%# `yield` (with no arguments) is the heart of a Rails layout: it's the
        spot where whatever the CURRENT PAGE's own template rendered gets
        inserted. For example, when visiting Settings, Rails first renders
        app/views/settings/index.html.erb into a block of HTML, then
        renders THIS layout file around it, substituting that HTML in right
        here. `<%= yield % >` both runs `yield` and prints its result. %>
    <%= yield %>
  <%# Closes the `<main>` element opened above. %>
  </main>

  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# FOOTER %>
  <%# ======================================================================= %>
  <%# `<footer>` is the semantic HTML tag for the page's closing/footer
      region. Styled with a top border, top margin to separate it from
      content above, vertical padding, centered small muted text. %>
  <footer class="border-t border-gray-800 mt-12 py-6 text-center text-xs text-gray-600">
    <%# Plain literal footer text. `&nbsp;` is an HTML entity for a
        non-breaking space (a space character that browsers won't
        collapse or line-wrap on), used here around the "|" separator
        purely for visual spacing. %>
    StorageFinder — self-hosted, runs on your LAN &nbsp;|&nbsp;
    <%# `Time.current` gets the current time (Rails-aware of the app's
        configured time zone). `.strftime("%b %d, %Y")` formats it as a
        string using strftime's format codes: `%b` = abbreviated month
        name (e.g. "Jul"), `%d` = zero-padded day of month, `%Y` =
        4-digit year — producing something like "Jul 18, 2026". %>
    <span>Last updated: <%= Time.current.strftime("%b %d, %Y") %></span>
  <%# Closes the `<footer>` element opened above. %>
  </footer>

<%# Closes the `<body>` element opened near the top of the file. %>
</body>
<%# Closes the `<html>` root element opened at the very top of the file. %>
</html>
